Create two instances and damage one.
```javascript
class Character {
  constructor(name, health) {
    this.name = name;
    this.health = health;
  }
  takeDamage(amount) {
    this.health = Math.max(0, this.health - amount);
  }
}
const fern = new Character("Fern", 14);
const ash = new Character("Ash", 11);
fern.takeDamage(4);
console.log(fern.health, ash.health);
```
The output is `10 11`. Calling fern's method makes this refer to fern. Ash's health property is a different field on a different instance and remains 11.

Here is an independently runnable large-damage example.
```javascript
class Character {
  constructor(name, health) {
    this.name = name;
    this.health = health;
  }
  takeDamage(amount) {
    this.health = Math.max(0, this.health - amount);
  }
}
const fern = new Character("Fern", 14);
fern.takeDamage(20);
console.log(fern.health);
fern.takeDamage(2);
console.log(fern.health);
```
Both outputs are 0. The first subtraction would be negative, so the maximum selects 0. Later damage also stays at the minimum.

