This example creates two characters and damages one.
```javascript
class Character {
  constructor(name, health) {
    this.name = name;
    this.health = health;
  }
  takeDamage(amount) {
    this.health = Math.max(0, this.health - amount);
  }
}
const fern = new Character("Fern", 14);
const ash = new Character("Ash", 11);
fern.takeDamage(4);
console.log(fern.health, ash.health);
```
Output:
```text
10 11
```
`fern.takeDamage(4)` changes Fern's health from 14 to 10. Ash keeps 11 because each character has its own health property.

Damage larger than the remaining health should leave zero.
```javascript
class Character {
  constructor(name, health) {
    this.name = name;
    this.health = health;
  }
  takeDamage(amount) {
    this.health = Math.max(0, this.health - amount);
  }
}
const fern = new Character("Fern", 14);
fern.takeDamage(20);
console.log(fern.health);
fern.takeDamage(2);
console.log(fern.health);
```
Output:
```text
0
0
```
The first hit uses up all 14 health. The next hit leaves it at zero.
